Dear Managers:

I receive two very fast responses to my request one coming from:
Alan Nabeth
Dr. Tom Blinn

And both suggestion point to a failure on the motherboard which may be
replaced for repairing.
The question is: There are motherboards to replace the damaged one?
I transcribe now the messages coming the the managers for you to know:
Dr. Tom Blinn wrote:

A machine check in PAL mode during power up is probably due to something

on the motherboard having failed.  You mentioned power problems, I
think,
in your first message.  Serious power fluctuations can fry components on

the motherboard.  It could be a plug in card, but that's unlikely.  The
motherboard is not field repairable -- you have to replace it as a unit
and then the dead part is sent to a repair depot.  I doubt that you have

a spare in your own shop.  Compaq Services might have one in your
country
if you are really lucky.  Otherwise it will have to be obtained from a
regional supply depot.

Good luck.

Alan Wrote:

That error is from early enough in the boot problem that
        it probably hasn't even touched a hard drive.  This is
        probably a CPU problem.

Many thanks to Alan and Tom, I hope to be lucky, with the replace parts.

One final an truly important issue is that the power poor quality
becomes the main responsible of the failure. We are asking the power
supply company for this issue.

