From tru64-unix-managers  Fri May 12 08:16:57 2000
From: Jon Morgan <jmorgan () dircon ! co ! uk>
Date: Fri, 12 May 2000 08:16:57 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Bug in v5.0 installupdate command
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079346819565

(I've cheated and called this a summary, when there was no real
question sent to list. Sorry about that.)

There is a bug in the v5.0 /sbin/installupdate shell script that
causes the upgrade from v5.0 -> v5.0a to fail. This script contains
the following lines:

        FSTAB_READONLY=`
        cat /etc/fstab |
        while read LINE
        do
                set -- $LINE
                for FS in /usr /var
                do
                        if [ "$2" = "$FS" ]
                        then
                            echo $4 | grep -q "ro" &&
                            {
                                        [ "$NEED_AND" ] && echo " and
\c"
                                        echo "$FS\c"
                                        NEED_AND=1
                            }
                        fi
                done
        done`

The grep in this command matches "groupquota" in the /etc/fstab file,
for example:

	# /etc/fstab

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
------
	#
	# Mount all the system specific partitions
	#
	root_domain#root        /               advfs
rw,userquota,groupquota 0 1
	/proc                   /proc           procfs
rw,userquota,groupquota 0 0
	usr_domain#usr          /usr            advfs
rw,userquota,groupquota 0 2
	usr_domain#var          /var            advfs
rw,userquota,groupquota 0 2
	#

I've logged this as a call with Compaq CSC in the UK and engineering
have acknowledged this as a bug. The fix is to either remove the check
from /sbin/installupdate, or to change the shell script to search for
the correct field.

Hope this helps,

		-jon.

--
jon morgan

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri May 12 06:39:19 2000
From: Albrecht Gebhardt <albrecht.gebhardt () uni-klu ! ac ! at>
Date: Fri, 12 May 2000 06:39:19 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: Followup4: Upgrade to 4.0E crashes DEC3000 when starting X
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079346819564


System:
DEC 3000 M800, 200MHz, 128MB, PMAGC-BA (= ZLX-M2)

Symptom:
cpu panic when the CDE login screen starts

Description:

This problem was posted one year ago. I found only three followups but no
summary with a solution. The originial posting can be found at:

http://www.ornl.gov/its/archives/mailing-lists/tru64-unix-managers/1999/06/msg00041.html 

What happens: X starts with blank screen, a corrupted mouse pointer shows
up (only a few pixels forming kind of a small line), dxconsole shows up
... and then the crash.

It turned out that this seems to be related with the pixmap graphic in the
cde login window:

I switched to xdm, then a corrupted (vertically striped) digital logo is
displayed in background but xdm worked. DXsession works, the same
corrupted mouse pointer, almost invisible but working. Starting the first
application containing pixmaps now reproduces the crash. (I used xemacs)

Workaround/Solution:

All followups and the originial posting suggest either to use a generic
kernel or to disable X logins.

I installed Open3D V. 4.93 with Open3D_DU40E_PatchKit4, still the same
problem.

No problems with genvmunix (as before), so I built my own genvmunix using
/sys/conf/GENERIC, it works also fine.

Now I checked what the extra options in my /sys/conf/MYSYSTEM compared to
/sys/conf/GENERIC are:

options         UERF
options         DLI
options         DLB
options         NTP_TIME
options         PCKT
options         DEC_ACL
options         PACKETFILTER

When I add all these to /sys/conf/GENERIC I get the same crash.

Now I removed the above options from my customized kernel
(/sys/conf/MYSYSTEM) and it works! Of course now I had no more LAT because
of removing DLB.

Now I tried subsequently reenabling these options and it turned out that I
can use UERF, NTP_TIME, PCKT and PACKETFILTER but have to avoid DLI, DLB
and DEC_ACL.

So my workaround boils down to: 
+ set XLOGIN to "" in rc.config to stay in console only mode
+ build a custom kernel without DEC ACLs
+ remove DLI and DLB from /sys/conf/SYSTEMNAME (if LAT was installed,
  not installing LAT should also solve this)
+ rebuild the kernel (doconfig -c SYSTEMNAME), move it to /vmunix
+ reenable XLOGIN="cde"

Note that I have not heavily tested this, I was just happy to have my
system running again. xdpyinfo reports the GLX extension. Of course I
compiled the GL examples and tested these, results are as expected (some
polygon errors as with DU4.0B+O3D before).
 


