Hello,

SUMMARY:
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The majority of the effort to battle a virus/worm has been spent in
preventing a virus/worm from entering a mail store via anti-virus
software or MTA (e.g., sendmail), and/or local delivery agent (e.g.
procmail) filters.  However, once the virus/worm has penetrated the
defenses, limited tools are available for the Digital UNIX and perhaps
most UNIX environments to clean up.  Due to the necessity of
requiring a virus/worm to exist in the wild prior to actually having a
filter/signature present, good clean up tools are necessary and
essential.

Several folks sent copies of their home grown scripts to clean up the
mbox format folder.  One of my main concerns is the tool must properly
lock the folder prior to making any modifications (e.g., flock(),
lockf(), fcntl(), and lock file [fcntl() may be most critical in a NFS
environment]).

Refer to the CERT advisory, CA-2000-04, about the Love Letter Worm for
recommendations on how to filter.  Visit
http://www.cert.org/advisories/CA-2000-04.html
------------------------------------------------------------------------

---
Regards,
Richard Jackson
Computer Center Lead Engineer,
Central Systems & Dept. UNIX Consulting
University Computing & Information Systems (UCIS)
George Mason University, Fairfax, Virginia


